<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Weight Loss: Points to Support Appetite" description="Acupressure is sometimes used to support weight management by calming cravings and aiding digestion. Learn the points used, with an honest look at the limited evidence." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Weight Loss", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-weight-loss/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-27" dateModified="2026-08-27" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Weight Loss: Points to Support Appetite and Digestion</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Some people explore acupressure as a supportive tool for weight management, hoping to calm cravings, aid digestion, and reduce stress-driven eating. This guide covers the points most often used for that purpose. It is important to be clear up front: acupressure is not a proven weight-loss method, and it works at most as a small support alongside diet, movement, and sleep.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><strong>Research Note:</strong> Studies on acupressure and acupuncture for weight management — much of it using ear (auricular) points — have produced mixed results. Some small trials report modest effects on appetite or weight, but the evidence is inconsistent and prone to placebo effects. Treat any result as supportive, never as a replacement for lifestyle change.</p></div><section id="cv12" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/cv12-zhongwan-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">1. CV12 (Zhongwan) - Middle of the Stomach</a></h2><p class="mb-3">CV12 sits on the upper abdomen and is traditionally used to regulate the stomach, calm bloating, and support healthy appetite signals.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the midline of the upper abdomen, about four finger-widths above the navel. <a href="/points/cv12-zhongwan-acupressure-point/">CV12 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press gently in slow circles for 2-3 minutes before meals, breathing deeply.</p></section><section id="st25" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/st25-tianshu-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">2. ST25 (Tianshu) - Celestial Pivot</a></h2><p class="mb-3">ST25 lies beside the navel and is traditionally used to regulate the intestines and support a comfortable, regular digestion.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> Two finger-widths out from the navel on each side. <a href="/points/st25-tianshu-acupressure-point/">ST25 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press gently in slow circles for 2-3 minutes on each side.</p></section><section id="st36" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">3. ST36 (Zusanli) - Leg Three Miles</a></h2><p class="mb-3">ST36 is the master digestive point and is traditionally used to strengthen digestion and steady energy, which can help reduce erratic hunger.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> About four finger-widths below the kneecap, one finger-width outward from the shinbone. <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/">ST36 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly for 2-3 minutes on each leg.</p></section><section id="sp6" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/sp6-sanyinjiao-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">4. SP6 (Sanyinjiao) - Three Yin Intersection</a></h2><p class="mb-3">SP6 is traditionally used to support digestion and fluid balance and appears in many routines aimed at metabolic and abdominal comfort.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the inside of the lower leg, about four finger-widths above the inner ankle bone. <a href="/points/sp6-sanyinjiao-acupressure-point/">SP6 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly for 2-3 minutes on each leg. Avoid during pregnancy.</p></section><section id="li11" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/li11-quchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">5. LI11 (Quchi) - Pool at the Bend</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LI11 at the elbow is traditionally used to clear heat and support the body's metabolic balance in TCM practice.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> At the lateral end of the elbow crease when the arm is bent. <a href="/points/li11-quchi-acupressure-point/">LI11 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly for 1-2 minutes on each arm.</p></section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="space-y-4"><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can acupressure make me lose weight?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">On its own, no. At best it may help you feel calmer and more in tune with hunger cues. Lasting weight change comes from diet, physical activity, sleep, and — when needed — medical support.</p></details><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How often should I do it?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">A few minutes on the abdominal and leg points once or twice a day, especially before meals, is a reasonable self-care habit. Stop if anything feels painful.</p></details></div></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This content is for informational purposes only and is not medical advice. Acupressure is not a treatment for obesity or a substitute for a clinician-guided weight-management plan.</p></div></article>
